3. Z PROBE
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
TRIGGER HEIGHT = -0.030mm  +/-0.005   (SLIP GAUGE, tight assembly, 21/08/2026)
Measured at bed centre with G31 Z0 and a fresh G30 datum, nozzle parked at Z1.100:
    1.06mm gauge  slips under with ZERO resistance   -> gap > 1.060
    1.07mm gauge  goes with a PUSH                   -> gap ~ 1.070
    contact = 1.100 - 1.070 = 0.030  ->  trigger height -0.030

HISTORY OF THIS VALUE
  +0.700  original placeholder - wrong in sign AND magnitude
  -0.025  paper feeler (0.10mm, micrometer), hotend screws LOOSE, +/-0.025
  -0.025  paper feeler repeated after tightening - IDENTICAL, so the loose joint
          was not in the probe's vertical load path
  -0.030  slip gauge, +/-0.005  <- CURRENT, in config.g

The paper method was sound: it landed within 5 microns of the slip gauge. The
gain from slip gauges was PRECISION (+/-0.025 -> +/-0.005), from bracketing with
two blocks 0.01 apart rather than a 0.05-wide guess. Paper compression, which was
the suspected error source, turned out to be negligible.

NEGATIVE is correct - the ALPS is a strain gauge, so the nozzle presses in before
the threshold is crossed. It is a CONSTANT offset: it moves the datum, never the
SHAPE of a heightmap. No mesh is invalidated by a change here.

*** STILL REDO AT PRINTING TEMPERATURE once the PT1000 is replaced. Nozzle
    thermal expansion shifts this 0.05-0.10mm - ten to twenty times the current
    uncertainty. Cold value is fine for geometry, must not reach printing. ***

Note the DTI cannot be used to check this. It is a dial TEST indicator (lever
type), so absolute readings carry cosine error that depends on stylus arm angle.
Good for nulling and for shape; not a length standard.

P5 vs P9  ->  RESOLVED: P9, and it is load-bearing.
RRF only auto-runs deployprobe.g / retractprobe.g for deployable probe types
(type 9). Those macros are what assert the ALPS enable pin (M42 P1 S1/S0).
Switch to P5 and the enable never asserts and the probe goes dead.
DO NOT "tidy" this to P5.

Probe output is BINARY (0 / 1000), confirmed by a 21-step scan at 0.01mm
intervals - it sits at 0 then snaps to 1000. So the P500 threshold is not
discriminating anything; any value 1..999 behaves identically.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. bed.g MOTOR ORDER BUG  (found and fixed)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
RRF pairs G30 P<n> to the n-th M671 coordinate. bed.g had P1 and P2 swapped
relative to M671, so G32 was applying the front-left correction to the rear
motor and vice versa.

Invisible on a near-level bed - all three corrections come out near-equal and
swapping two equal numbers changes nothing. It would only bite once the bed was
genuinely out of tram, as failure to converge.

Corner-to-driver mapping CONFIRMED PHYSICALLY by M584 single-driver jog:
  driver 0.0 = front-right   M671 entry 0 (424.75, -8.75)
  driver 0.1 = rear          M671 entry 1 (201, 415)
  driver 0.2 = front-left    M671 entry 2 (-22.75, -8.75)
config.g's comment was correct. project.txt was wrong and has been fixed.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. FRAME GEOMETRY SURVEY
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
METHOD
Bed is granite = rigid flat reference. Self-weight sag of a 20mm granite strip
over a 400mm span computes to 0.0053mm - fifty times too small to matter, so the
slab cannot be the source of anything measured here. A rigid plate on a 3-point
mount defines a plane exactly, so after G32 the entire heightmap residual is
MACHINE geometry, not bed.

HEIGHTMAP - RUN 3, THE VALID ONE (7x7, X10:390 Y16:380)
min 0.000  max 0.459  mean 0.137  deviation 0.104
Row 0 = Y16 (FRONT), row 6 = Y380 (REAR). RRF writes rows from Ymin upward.

   0.273  0.237  0.221  0.197  0.182  0.181  0.249     <- Y16   front
   0.151  0.108  0.082  0.078  0.071  0.071  0.153
   0.082  0.042  0.023  0.011  0.001  0.024  0.113
   0.069  0.028  0.008  0.000  0.009  0.029  0.129
   0.088  0.057  0.041  0.037  0.063  0.086  0.187
   0.156  0.121  0.117  0.131  0.149  0.189  0.306
   0.256  0.244  0.259  0.276  0.304  0.346  0.459     <- Y380  rear
  X10                                            X390

Positive = bed reads HIGH = toolhead sits LOW there (bed is flat granite, so it
is the gantry that moves). Sign confirmed by dropping the bed 0.400mm and
watching the DTI fall.

EDGE SPREADS (left to right, mean removed)   run1     run2     run3
  front edge                                -0.022   -0.018   -0.024
  rear  edge                                +0.419   +0.210   +0.203
  -> front reproduces everywhere. Rear was DOUBLED by the loose toolhead.

RAIL HEIGHTS (sign flipped, +ve = rail higher; columns X10 / X390)
     Y     left    right      R-L
    16   -0.273   -0.249   +0.024
    77   -0.151   -0.153   -0.002
   137   -0.082   -0.113   -0.031
   198   -0.069   -0.129   -0.060
   259   -0.088   -0.187   -0.099
   319   -0.156   -0.306   -0.150
   380   -0.256   -0.459   -0.203

Right rail drop ACCELERATES rearward - a uniform twist would be linear. This is
a droop or mislocated support at the rear, not the assembly being cocked.

Rail crown vs own chord: left +0.196, right +0.225 (common mode - ignore)
X beam bow after removing rail tilt: SUPERSEDED by section 5b - that figure was
cold-only and read off a single row.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5b. X BEAM - ISOLATED   (added 22/08/2026)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Method: remove the per-row linear X tilt from every row of the map (that tilt IS
the Y rail fault), then average the residual over all Y. What is left is the
beam's own shape.

SIGN HERE IS PHYSICAL, NOT HEIGHTMAP. + = gantry rides HIGH, - = gantry rides
LOW, referenced to a straight chord X10 -> X390. The heightmap sign is inverted
from the beam - an earlier note in this file said "cold dips / hot crowns", which
described the MAP. The BEAM crowns cold and sags hot.

  X position        10     73    137    200    263    327    390
  COLD (20C)      0.000 +0.046 +0.071 +0.086 +0.092 +0.083  0.000
  HOT  (46C frm)  0.000    --   -0.178   --   -0.152   --    0.000

                 peak      at X    direction
  cold          0.089mm    ~200    beam CROWNS UP,  gantry high mid-span
  hot           0.186mm    ~200    beam SAGS DOWN,  gantry low  mid-span
  swing         0.275mm            = 10.6 microns/K over dT 26K

End asymmetry negligible: right end 0.020mm below left cold, 0.008mm hot. Clean
symmetric arc centred on mid-travel.

IT REALLY IS THE BEAM. The detrended profile is identical at every Y - row-to-row
scatter 5.1 microns cold, 4.5 microns hot, against a 0.089-0.186mm signal. A bed
or rail artifact would vary with Y. This does not. Same bent member carried around.

COST, HOT, first-layer variation across a centred part:
  100mm wide  0.013mm     300mm wide  0.116mm
  200mm wide  0.052mm     380mm full  0.186mm
Small central parts barely see it. A full-width first layer sees the lot.
Hot the beam is ~59% the size of the Y rail fault. Once the rail is shimmed the
beam becomes the DOMINANT remaining error and the mesh carries all 0.186mm of it.

*** CORRECTION - THE MECHANISM FIRST WRITTEN HERE WAS WRONG ***
Originally written up as a vertical thermal gradient across the section. That
does not survive arithmetic: 0.22mm of bow needs 6.1 K top-to-bottom across 20mm
of aluminium, and holding that takes 366 W per metre of beam through the wall
conduction path (k=200, two 3mm side walls, 20mm path). Free convection supplies
about 1% of that. Dead theory.

LIKELY MECHANISM: BIMETALLIC STRIP. Steel MGN9 rail (alpha 11.5) bolted along an
aluminium beam (alpha 23). Fully-bonded upper bound for a 26K rise is 0.40mm of
bow; observed 0.275mm, consistent with a rail bolted at intervals that slips a
little between fixings. It also explains the "inversion" cleanly - the bimetal
term is monotonic in dT and always one sign, and the beam simply starts crowned
+0.089 and gets pushed through zero into a sag. Nothing reverses; a linear effect
crosses an offset.
  Direction constraint: heating SAGS it, which needs the higher-alpha aluminium
  net BELOW the steel. CHECK where that MGN9 actually sits relative to the beam
  neutral axis before treating this as settled.

WHY THE BEAM CANNOT BE SHIMMED OR PACKED (20x20x3 6063, I = 10132 mm^4,
EI = 6.99e8 N.mm^2, ~500mm span):
  - 0.7kg toolhead at mid-span sags it        0.026 mm
  - to make the observed 0.22mm you need      59 N (6 kg) at mid-span
    or an end moment of                       4.9 N.m
    or an axial length mismatch of            0.24 MICRONS
A quarter of a micron. The beam's shape is set by its end constraints, not by
where you pack it. There is no interface spanning the fault to shim against, and
a shim is a constant while the fault reverses sign - size it hot and you double
the error cold.

NOT DONE - SLACKEN / HEAT / RETIGHTEN (considered 22/08/2026, rejected)
Slackening the rail screws hot and retightening would move the stress-free
temperature from 20C to 46C. Slip needed is only 0.15mm total (+/-0.075 from
centre), well inside M3-in-3.5mm hole clearance. But it cannot reach zero: the
beam already crowns +0.089 cold, so it is not stress-free cold either, and that
built-in bow survives. Predicted result 0.186 sag -> 0.089 crown hot, a 52%
reduction with a sign flip. Rejected because it risks a joint currently holding
4.5 micron repeatability, it would disturb the rail's Y-plane straightness which
cannot be measured and which the mesh cannot correct, and the dual MGN9 sandwich
already on the project list cancels the couple by symmetry instead of re-datuming
it - same job done properly, and it also treats the 215 Hz torsional mode.
NOTE the mesh workflow is unaffected either way: the cold->hot delta IS the
bimetal term, 0.275mm, identical before and after. The only gain would have been
less magnitude for the mesh to carry hot.
If it is ever done: check first whether the rail is located longitudinally (dowel
or shoulder) or it will not slip at all; park and support the toolhead at one end
since the carriage loads an unsupported rail; retighten from the centre outward;
threadlock, because preload drops ~10% on cooling.

STILL WORTH DOING - THE LINEARITY TEST (cheap, 32 points)
Two 4x4 meshes at different chamber setpoints. If bimetal, mid-span sagitta is
proportional to frame dT - at ~33C frame expect ~0.093mm. If it is linear the
mechanism is settled and the beam is predictable at any temperature without
measuring it. If it is NOT linear, the mechanism is end-constraint or joint
rotation instead, and anything aimed at the rail is aimed at the wrong thing.
Costs one heating cycle, which section 9 already requires anyway.

DTI CROSS-CHECK - NOW OF LIMITED VALUE
A Mitutoyo lever DTI clamped to an M6 bolt in the hotend was swept over the bed
and agreed with run 1 to rms 0.019mm. That agreement is much weaker evidence than
it first appeared: the indicator was mounted on THE SAME LOOSE HOTEND, so both
instruments shared the defect. Treat the DTI sweep as corroborating run 1, which
is itself discarded. The DTI also reads ~11% high (see section 7).
What the DTI did establish soundly: the sign convention, and that the granite is
a valid flat reference.

NOT A LOOSE JOINT
Rear joint is an angle bracket with a large gusset and 4x M6, plus tower
fasteners outboard. 0.42mm is far too large for elastic deflection of that.
Measurements agree: zero hysteresis on reversal, and the reading returned to
-0.225 at Y15 after a full traverse. A joint working loose would not repeat to
5 microns. Conclusion: built-in assembly geometry.
0.42mm over 400mm is 1 part in 1000 - below what a square and rule will hold on
a frame this size.

