6. SHIM SCHEDULE   -  RIGHT Y RAIL ONLY   (from HOT geometry)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Basis: MGN12, 500mm rail, 25mm pitch, 12.5mm end distance, 20 holes. Fastened at
the two centre holes then every other hole outward = 10 fixings. Rail centre
assumed on the Y travel centre (machine_Y = rail_pos - 55). Scale 550/380 =
1.447 (rail centres vs probe span). Zeroed on fastener 1 - the front is level.

BUILD TO THESE. The machine only ever prints hot.

 #   rail mm   machine Y   exact    USE     build from          (cold, do not use)
 1      37.5      -17.5    0.000    0.00    (none - datum)        0.00
 2      87.5       32.5    0.049    0.05    0.05                  0.00
 3     137.5       82.5    0.103    0.10    0.1                   0.05
 4     187.5      132.5    0.162    0.15    0.1 + 0.05            0.10
 5     237.5      182.5    0.225    0.20    0.2                   0.10
 6     262.5      207.5    0.258    0.25    0.2 + 0.05            0.15
 7     312.5      257.5    0.328    0.35    0.2 + 0.1 + 0.05      0.20
 8     362.5      307.5    0.403    0.40    0.4                   0.25
 9     412.5      357.5    0.482    0.50    0.4 + 0.1             0.30
10     462.5      407.5    0.566    0.55    0.4 + 0.1 + 0.05      0.40

stock: 0.05mm x5, 0.1mm x5, 0.2mm x3, 0.4mm x3   (order 2x, they get remade)

Underlying fit, hot, unscaled (before x1.447), from 4 Y samples:
  shim(Y) = -0.01269 + 0.000670*Y + 6.436e-07*Y^2     residual 0.0040mm
Cold equivalent, for reference only:
  shim(Y) = -0.00239 + 0.000276*Y + 8.574e-07*Y^2

RULES
- Shims must span the FULL rail width (12mm). Washer-sized shims let the rail
  bridge and deform locally - defeats the point and wears the bearings.
- Grade it, never step it. Every fixing gets its own thickness.
- Fastener 1 gets nothing. All correction added going rearward.
- Do NOT shim the left rail. Do NOT try to shim the X beam - its error is
  thermal bending and it reverses sign. See section 5b.

CAVEATS
- Fasteners 1 and 10 (Y-17.5, Y+407.5) are EXTRAPOLATED beyond the Y16-380 the
  mesh covers. #10 is the largest in the stack.
- Hot profile comes from 4 Y samples (4x4 grid), not 7. The probe cannot do more
  hot. Fit residual is 4 microns so the quadratic is well determined, but it
  cannot see structure between those samples.
- Rail placement assumed, not measured. Low sensitivity (+/-20mm ~ 0.025mm).
- Rail spacing 550mm IS measured. Do not change without recomputing.
- After shimming, EVERYTHING here must be re-measured hot. The shim changes the
  geometry these numbers describe.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
7. Z SCALE DISCREPANCY  ***RESOLVED 21/08/2026 - Z IS CORRECT***
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Commanding a 0.400mm Z move produced 0.4425mm of DTI deflection (+10.6%). Two
candidates were open: wrong Z steps/mm, or the DTI over-reading.

RESOLVED BY DIRECT MEASUREMENT after a power cycle:
  commanded Z50 -> Z20   = 30.000mm
  caliper, fixed frame reference to bed surface:
      at Z50   81.97 mm
      at Z20   51.96 mm
      measured travel 30.01 mm   -> error 0.03%, i.e. caliper resolution

*** M92 Z800 IS CORRECT. The bed arm linkage theory is dead. ***
*** The heightmap is in REAL MILLIMETRES. The shim table in section 6 stands
    exactly as written - NO 10% rescale. ***

The 10.6% therefore belongs to the Mitutoyo, almost certainly a stylus that is
not the length the indicator is calibrated for (lever DTIs scale directly with
stylus length; a short stylus over-reads). Dividing every DTI reading by 1.1062
improves agreement with the mesh from rms 0.0231 to 0.0187mm - a 19% improvement,
supportive but not decisive on its own. The caliper check is the real evidence.

PRACTICAL CONSEQUENCE: that indicator reads ~11% high until the stylus is
identified. Fine for finding shape and nulling to zero; do not trust it for
absolute magnitudes. Check the stylus length against the indicator's rated length
before using it as a measuring instrument again.

One point remains a genuine outlier: front-right (X385 Y15) sits -0.043 from the
mesh as read, and -0.060 corrected - it gets WORSE with the correction, unlike
everything else. Front-left was never captured. Worth re-measuring both corners
before relying on the front edge being level to 0.022mm.

