9. NEXT STEPS, IN ORDER
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 1. RE-VERIFY THE HOT GEOMETRY on a fresh soak before cutting shims. The hot
    numbers come from one heating cycle. Cold took three runs to get right.
    Procedure: chamber to 60, wait for frame drift <0.12C/min (~35 min), G28,
    G32, then a 4x4 G29. Compare rear spread against +0.314.
 2. Shim the right rail per section 6. Requires removing rear insulation.
 3. Re-check the FRONT corners after - freeing a structural joint on a frame
    this size can redistribute more than intended.
 4. Re-run the whole hot survey. The shim changes the geometry these numbers
    describe, so everything above is superseded once metal goes in.
 5. printstart.g MUST be reworked before any real print. It currently homes and
    loads a mesh with no reference to chamber state. With a 0.877mm cold-to-hot
    datum shift, homing cold and printing hot puts the first layer badly out.
    It needs: preheat chamber -> soak -> G28 -> G32 -> coarse G29 -> print.
    NOT YET WRITTEN - deliberately left, 21/08/2026.
 6. Characterise a 5x5 hot mesh. 25 points sits right on the ALPS ceiling. If it
    completes reliably it is the better print-start grid than 4x4, which loses
    up to 65 microns of mid-span detail against a full 7x7.
 7. The ALPS point ceiling deserves its own session with the vendor's specs.
    ~25 points hot is a low limit for a heated-chamber machine.
 8. After the PT1000 arrives: hot trigger height recalibration, then re-mesh.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
9b. THERMAL BEHAVIOUR - MEASURED 21/08/2026
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Chamber 22.4 -> 59.6C in ~23 min including a door opening (top panel now fitted,
which project.txt still lists as outstanding).

FRAME LAGS AIR BADLY AND NEVER EQUALISES
  chamber air 60C  ->  frame settles ~46C.  A 14C gap held at equilibrium.
  The frame thermally bridges to outside the chamber and the machine exterior is
  5mm alloy sheet, so it sheds heat continuously. It approaches, never flattens.
  Soak criterion must be a RATE, not a plateau: wait for frame drift <0.12C/min.
  It took ~35 min from cold to meet that.
  Frame temperature is therefore a function of AMBIENT as well as setpoint -
  a cold workshop and a warm one give different geometry at the same setpoint.

FRAME TEMPERATURE IS MEASURABLE: the failed hotend PT1000 is a bare RTD now
foil-taped to a FRONT extrusion, reading on temp0. It is a proxy - it sits at
the good end of the frame, and the defect is at the rear, which may soak
differently. Consider a permanent frame channel on temp3 (free) later.

WHAT CHANGES BETWEEN COLD AND HOT
  right rail drop at rear   0.203 -> 0.314 mm   (+0.111, ~4 microns per degC)
  X beam mid-span curvature  inverts, ~0.2mm swing
  Z datum                    0.877 mm
  front rail differential    -0.024 -> -0.019   (unchanged, still the datum)

The rail differential change is monotonic in Y - zero at the front, growing to
+0.114 at the rear. Consistent with the rear support moving while the front
stays pinned: the same axis as the assembly error, amplified by heat.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
9c. SYNTHESISED HOT MESH - THE WORKING METHOD
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PROBLEM: the geometry that matters is HOT, but the ALPS cannot complete a fine
mesh hot (~21-25 point ceiling, section 1d). A fine COLD mesh is easy; a fine
HOT mesh is impossible. Probing hot at every print start is also slow.

SOLUTION (Trev's, 21/08/2026): measure the fine detail cold, measure the THERMAL
CORRECTION coarse, add them, and recall the result. Never probe a full mesh hot.

PROCEDURE
 1. Cold, machine at rest:   G28, G32, full 7x7 G29.   -> cold_7x7
 2. Heat chamber to print temp. Wait for FRAME drift <0.12C/min (temp0), NOT for
    chamber PV. Takes ~35 min from cold. Record the frame temperature.
 3. Hot:  G28, G32, then a 4x4 G29 (16 points).        -> hot_4x4
 4. Subsample cold_7x7 at indices 0,2,4,6 in both axes -> cold_4x4
    (the 4x4 grid points are an EXACT SUBSET of the 7x7 - X at 10/136.7/263.3/390,
     Y at 16/137.3/258.7/380 - so no registration error and no extra measurement)
 5. delta = hot_4x4 - cold_4x4, both mean-removed
 6. Bilinear-interpolate delta up to the 7x7 grid
 7. synthesised_hot_7x7 = cold_7x7 + interpolated delta
 8. Write as heightmap.csv. Recall at print start with G29 S1.

*** USE 16 POINTS (4x4), NOT 4 (2x2). ***
A 2x2 grid has only four degrees of freedom - constant, X tilt, Y tilt, twist.
The single largest thermal effect is the X beam INVERTING mid-span, which is
quadratic and completely invisible from corners. Corners alone would report the
front edge simply dropping and miss a 0.2mm crown between them:
    front row, mean-removed    X10     X137    X263    X390
    cold                      +0.042  -0.010  -0.049  +0.018
    hot                       -0.079  +0.108  +0.068  -0.097
                              corners see these, and miss the middle entirely.

MEASURED CORRECTION SURFACE, 21/08/2026 (46C frame, 60C chamber, temp slab):
   -0.012  +0.222  +0.226  -0.007      Y16 front
   -0.062  +0.189  +0.217  -0.027
   -0.162  +0.118  +0.129  -0.071
   -0.361  -0.093  -0.053  -0.250      Y380 rear
   X10     X136.7  X263.3  X390
Two effects superimposed: middle columns rise ~0.22 (beam crowning) while the
rear-left corner drops 0.36 (rail differential). Verified by reconstruction -
synthesised edge spreads match measured hot exactly (front -0.020, rear +0.314).

THREE THINGS THIS DOES NOT SOLVE
 a) DATUM. G29 S1 restores the SHAPE, not Z0, and the datum moves 0.877mm from
    cold to hot. Print start MUST re-datum hot - a G32, or at minimum a G30, once
    soaked. That is 3-4 probe points, trivial against the ~25 ceiling.
 b) TEMPERATURE VALIDITY. The correction belongs to the frame temperature it was
    measured at. temp0 (bare RTD foil-taped to a front extrusion) gives a frame
    reading - print start should check the frame is near that value before
    trusting a recalled map. Frame temp depends on AMBIENT as well as setpoint,
    so a cold workshop and a warm one are not the same condition.
 c) INTERPOLATION ERROR, UNQUANTIFIABLE. Validating it needs a hot 7x7, which is
    precisely what the probe cannot deliver. For scale: reconstructing the cold
    7x7 from its own 4x4 subset gives 0.026mm rms / 0.065mm worst. The correction
    surface may be smoother than the absolute surface, or it may not be. Unknown.

A 5x5 hot mesh would shrink (c) but sits right on the probe ceiling - see
section 9 step 6.

 d) *** OPEN: CHAMBER HEAT vs NOZZLE HEAT. *** The 0.877mm datum shift and the
    whole correction surface were measured with a COLD NOZZLE - the PT1000 has
    failed mechanically so the hotend cannot be run. Real printing adds nozzle
    and heatbreak expansion: ~30-40mm of steel/brass over 20->280C is order
    0.1mm more. Small against 0.877 but half a first layer.
    OPERATIONALLY IT DOES NOT MATTER provided the datum is taken at FULL
    operating condition - chamber soaked AND nozzle at temperature. One G30 then
    captures both and the split is academic.
    It only bites this method if nozzle heat changes the SHAPE rather than just
    the datum. Expectation is datum-only (tip expansion is a pure Z offset, and
    a moving toolhead should not impose a static pattern on the beam) but that is
    reasoning, not measurement.
    TEST WHEN THE PT1000 IS FITTED: take the 4x4 correction twice, once
    nozzle-cold and once nozzle-hot, and difference them. If it is a constant
    offset, the correction surface stands as measured.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
9d. WHY THIS MACHINE NEEDS A MESH AND A 160mm ONE DOES NOT
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Trev's Saladfork (160x160, also ALPS) needs no mesh at all - home cold, tram hot,
re-establish Z hot, done. That is the correct practice there and insufficient
here, and the reason is span, not build quality.

The residual after tramming is SMOOTH and QUADRATIC, so it scales as span^2:

  measured, this machine, hot, 380x364mm      range 0.548 mm
  same surface over a central ~127x121mm      range 0.059 mm

  extrapolated:  160mm span -> 0.088 mm
                 250mm span -> 0.214 mm
                 400mm span -> 0.548 mm

At 160mm the residual is inside first-layer tolerance and a mesh buys nothing.
At 400mm the same geometry gives six times the deviation and a mesh is required.
Quadrupling the bed area does not quadruple the problem - it sextuples it.



-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
10. THINGS THAT WOULD HAVE CAUGHT ME OUT
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
*** A LOOSE TOOLHEAD SILENTLY CORRUPTS EVERY PROBE MEASUREMENT. ***
The ALPS is a strain gauge - it measures force THROUGH the toolhead, so any loose
joint sits in the load path. Loose hotend screws doubled the apparent rear-edge
tilt (0.203 -> 0.419mm) and it did NOT look like noise: it looked like smooth,
plausible, self-consistent geometry that survived a decomposition and matched a
dial indicator. It nearly resulted in a rail being shimmed to correct for it.
CHECK TOOLHEAD FASTENERS BEFORE ANY PROBE SURVEY. And note the DTI was clamped
to the same hotend, so a second instrument agreeing is NOT independent
confirmation if it shares the mounting.

- RRF heightmap rows run Ymin FIRST. Row 0 is the FRONT, not the rear.
- The ALPS enable is de-asserted between probes by retractprobe.g. Reading
  sensors.probes[0].value outside a probe cycle returns 0 regardless of load -
  assert M42 P1 S1 first or the reading is meaningless.
- Heightmap "positive" means bed high, which with a flat bed means the GANTRY IS
  LOW there. The correction is to RAISE that corner, not lower it.
- move.compensation type must read "none" before any geometry measurement, or
  the machine is silently correcting the thing you are trying to measure.
- move.calibration.initial.deviation reads 0 both when G32 has never run and
  when it converged perfectly. It cannot distinguish them.
- bed.g exits its loop on convergence OR the 15-iteration cap. Completing G32 is
  NOT proof it converged.
- The final centre G30 in bed.g had NO result check until 21/08. It failed once
  ("probe already triggered before probing move started"), left the Z datum
  unset, and displaced a whole heightmap by 0.25mm while reporting success.
  Fixed: Z5 lift + 1s release dwell + result check. Do not remove them.
- EVERY conclusion that survived this survey was measured at least TWICE. Every
  one that did not survive - run 1's rear tilt, the probe-compliance theory, the
  suspicion that the hot interior inversion was probe bias - rested on a single
  measurement. Measure twice before acting, always. It is cheap and it has been
  decisive three separate times.
- LOOSE TOOLHEAD ALSO INVALIDATES INPUT SHAPING. The accelerometer is on the
  toolhead, so a loose hotend is a spring-mass right next to the sensor. The
  M593 P"zvd" F127 tuning of 19/08/2026 - and its validation figures (X ring-down
  rms 0.12 -> 0.08, 121Hz peak 0.14 -> 0.03) - were all measured through the loose
  joint. Re-run the whole characterisation from scratch, do not just retune F.
  The 127Hz "structural gantry mode" may shift, or may turn out to have been two
  modes blurred together.
- A repeatable measurement can still be a WRONG measurement. Run 1 was smooth,
  self-consistent, survived a polynomial decomposition and matched a dial
  indicator - and was still double the truth, because the toolhead was loose and
  the indicator was bolted to the same loose toolhead.
- ALWAYS take a second heightmap after a power cycle before acting on the first.
  That single practice caught all of the above.

